# naked_fn_attribute.tcyr — v6.2.27 (#naked attribute), completed v6.2.28
#
# Gates the #naked fn-attribute — frameless interrupt-handler emit (no
# prologue/epilogue/frame), token 133. What this covers:
#  1. Annotation-fork-desync: token 133 (#naked; 128 was already f64v_dot) must be
#     consumed in pass-1 + pass-2 of ALL forks, else a #naked fn drops into a
#     fork's catchall → "unexpected enum" on that target. check.sh compiles this
#     corpus on x86 + cross-OS (ecb/cass/pi), so a missed fork fails the build there.
#  2. A REAL ISR body: `asm { iretq }` — v6.2.28 corrected the v6.2.27 "cyrius has
#     no inline asm" mistake (asm{} blocks + the iretq mnemonic shipped long ago;
#     aarch64 eret was the only gap, added at .28). So #naked + asm{} = a writable
#     interrupt handler: frameless emit, body self-terminates in iretq/eret.
#  3. The DCE survival: isr_probe is non-underscore + referenced only by address
#     (&isr_probe below — an ISR goes in the IDT by address, never called), which
#     the DCE bitmap would normally stub. v6.2.28 forces a #naked fn reachable so
#     its real body emits AND its safety guards run.
#  4. The safety guards (verified manually + by negative probes at the cut): a
#     #naked fn with a parameter -> hard error "a #naked fn cannot have parameters";
#     a `return` in a #naked fn -> hard error "return is not allowed in a #naked fn".
#     A valid naked ISR is paramless, returns nothing, ends in asm.

include "lib/assert.cyr"

#naked fn isr_probe() {
    asm {
        iretq
    }
}

fn main(): i64 {
    # &isr_probe = the IDT-style address reference that keeps the naked ISR alive
    # through DCE while never calling it; main keeps its own frame either way.
    var idt_slot = &isr_probe;
    assert_neq(idt_slot, 0, "a #naked ISR with an asm{iretq} body emits + is address-referencable");
    var r = assert_summary();
    return r;
}
